The VFS layer of coole
======================

coole reads and writes every file through a small virtual file system
switch: the mc_*() functions of interface.c (mc_open, mc_read, mc_stat,
...) take a vfs_path_t (path.c) and hand the call to the class that
serves the path.  coole has one class, the local file system
(src/vfs/local/local.c), registered by vfs_plugins_init()
(src/vfs/plugins_init.c).

The layer is kept because the editor and the rest of the code are
written against it: paths carry their encoding (the #enc: element,
see vfs_get_encoding()), the current directory is kept by the layer
(vfs_get_current_dir(), vfs_setup_work_dir()), and temporary files
come from mc_tmpdir() and mc_mkstemps().

Rules for the code here:

* Exported symbols are declared in vfs.h, path.h, utilvfs.h or gc.h and
  begin with 'vfs_' or 'mc_'.

* A class is a struct vfs_class filled in by vfs_init_class() and its
  own operations, and registered with vfs_register_class().  An
  operation it does not have stays NULL: the switch answers ENOTSUP.

* gc.c frees what a class keeps open once nothing refers to it any
  more (vfs_stamp_create(), vfs_expire()).  The local class keeps
  nothing, but the machinery is what mc_chdir() relies on.
